case_summary.legal_issues:
2. Detenu filed a petition for a writ of Habeas Corpus under
Article 22
of the Constitution in the High Court of Judicature at Bombay. Before the High Court, three contentions were advanced on behalf of the detenu. They were :
(1) It was a case of solitary incident, and prosecution was pending;
(2) The detention order was malafide having been served to prevent ' I the Magistrate from passing an order on the application for leaving India;
(3) All material documents relevant for subjective satisfaction were not considered and the order has been passed mechanically. State of Maharashtra and Anr. (1981) 3 SCC 556 held after referring to. the rules of business of the Government of Maharashtra that both the Home Secretary and the Home Minister could act on behalf of the State Government and, therefore, it is immaterial whether the detaining authority or the Home Minister disposed of the representation. The present case is governed by the same rules of business and, there fore, the contention would stand negatived by the decisions in the aforementioned two cases. 8. It was next contended that even though the detenu retracted Ms confessional statement the same was not considered by the detaining 50 authority and in any event the same was not forwarded to the Advisory Board and, therefore, the detention order is vitiated. In para 11 of the writ petition it is alleged that the detenu retracted his confessional statement by his letter dated 16/19 December 1981 forwarded through the jail
